{
  "image_name": "3b98acf55cbff35861cdad9ef637ebed.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how telomerase, especially its catalytic subunit TERT, is activated in cancer and how it can be targeted therapeutically. At the center is the telomerase complex, made of TERT and the RNA template TERC, which drives telomere synthesis by adding repeat sequences to chromosome ends. The diagram shows that telomerase activity is increased through overexpression of TERT mRNA, which can result from promoter mutations, amplification or translocation, viral integration, and other mechanisms.\n\nSeveral treatment strategies are organized around this pathway. Direct inhibition of telomerase is shown with an anti-TERC oligonucleotide and a small-molecule TERT inhibitor, both blocking the telomerase complex. A separate approach uses G-quadruplex stabilizers that appear to act at telomeres by promoting or maintaining a quadruplex structure, thereby interfering with telomere extension. Immunologic targeting is also highlighted: telomerase vaccination stimulates antigen-presenting cells and CD8+ cytotoxic T cells to generate an anti-tumor immune response. In addition, high TERT expression can be exploited by TERT-driven oncolytic viruses or apoptotic proteins, leading to tumor cell lysis. Overall, the figure presents telomerase as both a key cancer-enabling mechanism and a versatile therapeutic target.",
